"Computation and Language (https://arxiv.org/list/cs.CL/new)\nciteturn41282view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/list/cs.CL/new\",\"pattern\":\"2610.22507\"}); Total lines: 2886\nL35: [2] cite18†arXiv:2610.22454 [cite19†pdf , cite20†html , cite21†other ]\nL36: \nL37: \nL38: Title: Where Prefill Expert Reduction Hurts MoE Models: A Few Prompt Tokens Carry Most of the Short-Answer F1 Loss\nL39: \nL40: cite22†Zhiyuan Wang L41: \nL42: Comments: 8 pages main text, 26 pages with appendices; supplementary material in ancillary files\nL43: \nL44: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL45: Running prompt tokens through fewer routed experts during prefill is a simple way to make mixture-of-experts (MoE) serving cheaper, yet it is unclear what this damages. We locate the damage with token-, layer- and attention-path interventions on short-answer question answering in four MoE families. At half the experts the F1 loss of three families is mostly answer form: the model still finds the answer but stops answering briefly.\nL46: In Qwen3-30B-A3B this loss is made at the two chat-template tokens before the answer, and in DeepSeek-V2-Lite at the first 8-32 prompt tokens, where its instruction sits; by answer likelihood these positions account for about half and a fifth of the damage. Reduction changes the first token's attention mass, norm and largest activation by less than 4%, and restoring super experts or massive activations recovers at most about a quarter of the loss, well below keeping the sensitive positions full.\nL47: On real prompts with about 4% of tokens at full experts, placing them on the task interface keeps F1 within one point of the unmodified model, while placing them at random in the documents is about 3 points worse (two of five prespecified primary comparisons; the others were undecided or not run).\nL48: Because the answer boundary always ends a single-turn prompt, and DeepSeek's loss was no longer detectable once its instruction moved away from the start, the sensitive tokens lay at the prompt ends in every sensitive layout we found, so rules that keep the ends are not distinguished from rules that read application marks. Time to first token falls about 14% on 11K-token prompts and not at 2K tokens.\nL49: [3] cite23†arXiv:2610.22468 [cite24†pdf , cite25†html , cite26†other ]\nL50: \nL51: \nL52: Title: From Parallels to Divergence: Language Models' Learning of Action Nominal Constructions\nL53: \nL54: cite27†Iskar Deng , cite28†Shane Steinert-Threlkeld L55: \nL56: Comments: 41 pages, including appendices\nL57: \nL58: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL59: Action nominal constructions (ANCs) express events in nominal form, with their argument marking following patterns found in clauses or ordinary noun phrases. In this paper, we investigate how different grammatical configurations affect language models' learning of ANCs and related constructions, and how these learning preferences compare with typological distributions in natural languages.\nL60: We use a grammar-engineered method for synthetic corpus construction that combines shared semantic input from natural corpora with computational grammars. We construct 96 target languages varying in clause word order, noun-phrase word order, alignment, complement system, and ANC strategy. For each language, we train GPT-2-small models from scratch and evaluate them using targeted minimal pairs covering 34 grammatical phenomena.\nL61: The results show typological parallels in basic clause configurations but divergences in complement systems and ANC strategies, with models also learning combinations rare or unattested in natural languages. This partial alignment suggests that natural language distributions may reflect both general learning pressures and functional demands not fully captured by model learning experiments.\nL62: [4] cite29†arXiv:2610.22507 [cite30†pdf , cite31†html , cite32†other ]\nL63: \nL64: \nL65: Title: RealCLI: Toward a Unified Evaluation of Human-Agent Collaboration for CLI Agents\nL66: cite33†Yunqi Liu , cite34†Peiyu Wang , cite35†Bingli Wang , cite36†Leo Luo , cite37†Xuanwu Song , cite38†Xudong Ma , cite39†Hongxin Li , cite40†Yaxin Du , cite41†Weicheng Gu , cite42†Fanglin Xu , cite43†Yi Xing , cite44†XiangLong Liu , cite45†Jujie He , cite46†Zhange Zhang , cite47†Huatong Song , cite48†Zhaoyi Li , cite49†Deyang Kong , cite50†Chuan Hao , cite51†Peihao Wu , cite52†Mingjie Tang , cite53†Ran Tao , cite54†Jian Yang , cite55†Wayne Xin Zhao , cite56†Bryan Dai L67: \nL68: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL69: Command-line interface (CLI) agents increasingly execute longer-horizon tasks, yet existing benchmarks either assume that user instructions remain fixed throughout execution or evaluate task outcomes without capturing runtime collaboration quality. In this paper, we present REALCLI, a paired benchmark that unifies the evaluation of task execution and human-agent collaboration quality on realistic CLI tasks.\nL70: To comprehensively measure collaboration, REALCLI builds a five-dimensional framework, operationalized into 22 sub-dimensions and 51 interaction scenario templates. REALCLI provides two modes for paired comparison: REALCLI-EXEC supplies complete specifications upfront, whereas REALCLI-COLLAB introduces a scripted user that delivers progressive information, corrections, constraint revisions, and authorization decisions.\nL71: Evaluations reveal profound capability gaps in frontier agents: the best configuration attains an average score of only 49.2%, and when evaluated by the joint collaboration score ($J_{\\mathrm{collab}}$), the peak performance reaches merely 37.6%. These results show that task effectiveness diverges sharply from collaboration quality, with models consistently struggling in steerability, trust calibration, and safety boundary management.\nL72: REALCLI provides a rigorous, reproducible foundation for measuring and advancing collaborative CLI agents.\nL73: [5] cite57†arXiv:2610.22511 [cite58†pdf , cite59†html , cite60†other ]\nL74: \nL75: \nL76: Title: AuditKIT: A Unified Evaluation Spine for Benchmarking, Judging, and Comparing Large Language Models and Agentic Systems\nL77: \nL78: cite61†Shreeyans Arora , cite62†Utsav Avaiya , cite63†Zera Lyngkhoi , cite64†Hem Gosalia , cite65†Ram Mohan Rao Kadiyala , cite66†Vinay Kumar Sankarapu , cite67†Pratinav Seth L79: Comments: 25 pages, 2 figures, 13 tables. Code: cite68†this https URL†github.com . Shreeyans Arora and Utsav Avaiya contributed equally; work done while at Lexsi Labs\nL80: \nL81: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Information Retrieval (cs.IR); Software Engineering (cs.SE)\nL82: Evaluating language models and agents requires combining benchmark accuracy, judge-based assessment, runtime performance, tool-use behavior, and retrieval quality. Differences in interfaces, metric semantics, and trace formats complicate these comparisons and can hide failures in agentic workflows.\nL83: We present AuditKIT, a source-available evaluation library that records these measurements in one result representation, with direction-aware metric contracts, configuration fingerprints, and extensible metrics and backends. For agents, it provides turn-aware tool-call matching, separates parallel from sequential execution, and distinguishes verified task outcomes from trajectory-level diagnostics. Missing or unobservable evidence is marked unscored rather than counted as failure.\nL84: For retrieval-augmented generation, it combines ranked retrieval metrics, judged grounding, and deterministic evidence checks. Seven case studies spanning model comparison, serving runtimes, retrieval, tool calling, and interoperability show how answer extraction, runtime behavior, and metric selection change evaluation outcomes.\nL85: [6] cite69†arXiv:2610.22515 [cite70†pdf , cite71†html , cite72†other ]\nL86: \nL87: \nL88: Title: alkhder at ImageEval 2026 Shared Tasks: Constrained Selection and Rotation-Averaged Logit Scoring for Multimodal Hallucination Detection\nL89: \nL90: cite73†Maher Pasa , cite74†Maria Hanini , cite75†Amro Najjar , cite76†Hasan Alkhder L91: \nL92: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL93: We describe our submission to Subtask~1b of ImageEval 2026. The task consists of selecting the single visually valid statement from three candidates paired with an image, and is in both English and Modern Standard Arabic (MSA). Our system uses a pre-trained 7B vision--language model in a zero-shot setting, without task-specific training or fine-tuning. We formulate the task as a constrained selection problem and choose the answer using next-token log-probabilities.\nL94: To reduce answer-position bias, we apply a Latin-square rotation to the candidate statements. Our system achieves CI scores of 0.0510 on the English test set and 0.0960 on the MSA test set, reducing CI relative to the official 3B baselines reported on devtest by 83.7\\% and 80.4\\%, respectively.\nL95: [7] cite77†arXiv:2610.22518 [cite78†pdf , cite79†html , cite80†other ]\nL96: \nL97: \nL98: Title: Learning User Representations to Improve Simulation Fidelity\nL99: \nL100: cite81†Shuhaib Mehri , cite82†Philippe Laban , cite83†Michel Galley , cite84†Dilek Tür , cite85†Jennifer Neville , cite86†Jianfeng Gao L101: \nL102: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL103: User simulators enable interactive training and evaluation of AI assistants at scale. Yet current simulators struggle to reproduce the diverse behaviors of real users, leading to assistants that generalize poorly to underrepresented users. We identify user representations as an underexplored bottleneck in user simulation. User representations are natural-language descriptions that condition simulators to reproduce a particular user's behavior.\nL104: A representation's quality becomes apparent in hindsight, through the behavior it induces in a simulator. To evaluate and optimize user representations, we formulate a conversation reconstruction task: given an original conversation, we generate a representation, condition a simulator on it, simulate a conversation, and then measure fidelity by comparing user behavior in the original and simulated conversations.\nL105: This comparison also yields hindsight feedback, which identifies where the representation falls short and how it can be improved. We introduce hindsight distillation, an online training procedure that distills feedback-conditioned improvements into the representation generator, enabling it to generate improved representations directly from the original conversation. We present UserRepGen-4B, a representation generator trained with hindsight distillation.\nL106: Across 700 conversations spanning seven datasets, hindsight feedback helps improve user representations at test time, and UserRepGen-4B generates representations that enable more faithful simulation than those generated by GPT-5.5. Beyond conversation reconstruction, learned representations improve simulation fidelity for unseen tasks, better preserve the population-level diversity of real users, and better distinguish users across conversations.\nL107: [8] cite87†arXiv:2610.22525 [cite88†pdf , cite89†html , cite90†other ]\nL108: \nL109: \nL110: Title: alkhder at AlexandriaX-2026: Context-Aware English-to-Dialectal Arabic Dialogue Translation with QLoRA-Fine-Tuned NileChat-3B\nL111: \nL112: cite76†Hasan Alkhder , cite73†Maher Pasa , cite74†Maria Hanini , cite75†Amro Najjar , cite91†Ahmet Zengin L113: \nL114: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComputation and Language (https://arxiv.org/list/cs.CL/new)\nciteturn41282view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/list/cs.CL/new\",\"pattern\":\"2610.22511\"}); Total lines: 2886\nL59: Action nominal constructions (ANCs) express events in nominal form, with their argument marking following patterns found in clauses or ordinary noun phrases. In this paper, we investigate how different grammatical configurations affect language models' learning of ANCs and related constructions, and how these learning preferences compare with typological distributions in natural languages.\nL60: We use a grammar-engineered method for synthetic corpus construction that combines shared semantic input from natural corpora with computational grammars. We construct 96 target languages varying in clause word order, noun-phrase word order, alignment, complement system, and ANC strategy. For each language, we train GPT-2-small models from scratch and evaluate them using targeted minimal pairs covering 34 grammatical phenomena.\nL61: The results show typological parallels in basic clause configurations but divergences in complement systems and ANC strategies, with models also learning combinations rare or unattested in natural languages. This partial alignment suggests that natural language distributions may reflect both general learning pressures and functional demands not fully captured by model learning experiments.\nL62: [4] cite29†arXiv:2610.22507 [cite30†pdf , cite31†html , cite32†other ]\nL63: \nL64: \nL65: Title: RealCLI: Toward a Unified Evaluation of Human-Agent Collaboration for CLI Agents\nL66: cite33†Yunqi Liu , cite34†Peiyu Wang , cite35†Bingli Wang , cite36†Leo Luo , cite37†Xuanwu Song , cite38†Xudong Ma , cite39†Hongxin Li , cite40†Yaxin Du , cite41†Weicheng Gu , cite42†Fanglin Xu , cite43†Yi Xing , cite44†XiangLong Liu , cite45†Jujie He