62†Utsav Avaiya , cite63†Zera Lyngkhoi , cite64†Hem Gosalia , cite65†Ram Mohan Rao Kadiyala , cite66†Vinay Kumar Sankarapu , cite67†Pratinav Seth L79: Comments: 25 pages, 2 figures, 13 tables. Code: cite68†this https URL†github.com . Shreeyans Arora and Utsav Avaiya contributed equally; work done while at Lexsi Labs\nL80: \nL81: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Information Retrieval (cs.IR); Software Engineering (cs.SE)\nL82: Evaluating language models and agents requires combining benchmark accuracy, judge-based assessment, runtime performance, tool-use behavior, and retrieval quality. Differences in interfaces, metric semantics, and trace formats complicate these comparisons and can hide failures in agentic workflows.\nL83: We present AuditKIT, a source-available evaluation library that records these measurements in one result representation, with direction-aware metric contracts, configuration fingerprints, and extensible metrics and backends. For agents, it provides turn-aware tool-call matching, separates parallel from sequential execution, and distinguishes verified task outcomes from trajectory-level diagnostics. Missing or unobservable evidence is marked unscored rather than counted as failure.\nL84: For retrieval-augmented generation, it combines ranked retrieval metrics, judged grounding, and deterministic evidence checks. Seven case studies spanning model comparison, serving runtimes, retrieval, tool calling, and interoperability show how answer extraction, runtime behavior, and metric selection change evaluation outcomes.\nL85: [6] cite69†arXiv:2610.22515 [cite70†pdf , cite71†html , cite72†other ]\nL86: \nL87: \nL88: Title: alkhder at ImageEval 2026 Shared Tasks: Constrained Selection and Rotation-Averaged Logit Scoring for Multimodal Hallucination Detection\nL89: \nL90: cite73†Maher Pasa , cite74†Maria Hanini , cite75†Amro Najjar , cite76†Hasan Alkhder L91: \nL92: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL93: We describe our submission to Subtask~1b of ImageEval 2026. The task consists of selecting the single visually valid statement from three candidates paired with an image, and is in both English and Modern Standard Arabic (MSA). Our system uses a pre-trained 7B vision--language model in a zero-shot setting, without task-specific training or fine-tuning. We formulate the task as a constrained selection problem and choose the answer using next-token log-probabilities.\nL94: To reduce answer-position bias, we apply a Latin-square rotation to the candidate statements. Our system achieves CI scores of 0.0510 on the English test set and 0.0960 on the MSA test set, reducing CI relative to the official 3B baselines reported on devtest by 83.7\\% and 80.4\\%, respectively.\nL95: [7] cite77†arXiv:2610.22518 [cite78†pdf , cite79†html , cite80†other ]\nL96: \nL97: \nL98: Title: Learning User Representations to Improve Simulation Fidelity\nL99: \nL100: cite81†Shuhaib Mehri , cite82†Philippe Laban , cite83†Michel Galley , cite84†Dilek Tür , cite85†Jennifer Neville , cite86†Jianfeng Gao L101: \nL102: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL103: User simulators enable interactive training and evaluation of AI assistants at scale. Yet current simulators struggle to reproduce the diverse behaviors of real users, leading to assistants that generalize poorly to underrepresented users. We identify user representations as an underexplored bottleneck in user simulation. User representations are natural-language descriptions that condition simulators to reproduce a particular user's behavior.\nL104: A representation's quality becomes apparent in hindsight, through the behavior it induces in a simulator. To evaluate and optimize user representations, we formulate a conversation reconstruction task: given an original conversation, we generate a representation, condition a simulator on it, simulate a conversation, and then measure fidelity by comparing user behavior in the original and simulated conversations.\nL105: This comparison also yields hindsight feedback, which identifies where the representation falls short and how it can be improved. We introduce hindsight distillation, an online training procedure that distills feedback-conditioned improvements into the representation generator, enabling it to generate improved representations directly from the original conversation. We present UserRepGen-4B, a representation generator trained with hindsight distillation.\nL106: Across 700 conversations spanning seven datasets, hindsight feedback helps improve user representations at test time, and UserRepGen-4B generates representations that enable more faithful simulation than those generated by GPT-5.5. Beyond conversation reconstruction, learned representations improve simulation fidelity for unseen tasks, better preserve the population-level diversity of real users, and better distinguish users across conversations.\nL107: [8] cite87†arXiv:2610.22525 [cite88†pdf , cite89†html , cite90†other ]\nL108: \nL109: \nL110: Title: alkhder at AlexandriaX-2026: Context-Aware English-to-Dialectal Arabic Dialogue Translation with QLoRA-Fine-Tuned NileChat-3B\nL111: \nL112: cite76†Hasan Alkhder , cite73†Maher Pasa , cite74†Maria Hanini , cite75†Amro Najjar , cite91†Ahmet Zengin L113: \nL114: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL115: This paper describes team Alkhder's submission to Subtask 1 of the AlexandriaX-2026 shared task, which asks systems to translate English dialogue turns into thirteen Arabic dialects. We fine-tune NileChat-3B-Base with QLoRA on the Alexandria parallel dialogue corpus, training one set of adapter weights for all dialects. Each turn is translated conditioned on the target country and dialect, the domain, the participant roles, the speaker-addressee gender direction, and the preceding dialogue context.\nL116: At inference, conversations are translated autoregressively and the model is fed its own previous outputs back as context without using any gold target-side test information. Under the constrained track, using only Alexandria data, it trains in about one hour and translates the private test set in under two hours. The system reaches a country-macro-averaged spBLEU of 20.36 and chrF++ of 36.64 on both tracks.\nL117: [9] cite92†arXiv:2610.22526 [cite93†pdf , cite94†html , cite95†other ]\nL118: \nL119: \nL120: Title: Literal Lock-In: How Test Language Shifts Literal Attraction in Typed Decision Models on Bengali Idioms\nL121: \nL122: cite96†Adib Sakhawat , cite97†Syed Rifat Raiyan L123: \nL124: Comments: Under review, 33 pages, 12 figures, 22 tables\nL125: \nL126: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL127: Decision models, non-generative scorers that report a probability over caller-supplied options and emit no text, are increasingly deployed as routers, classifiers and guardrails. Because they generate nothing, rationales and chain-of-thought do not apply. We audit three: an encoder scorer, a decoder scorer and a closed API, entirely through interventions on their input, using expert-annotated Bengali idioms carrying a gold figurative and a gold literal gloss.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComputation and Language (https://arxiv.org/list/cs.CL/new)\nciteturn41282view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/list/cs.CL/new\",\"pattern\":\"2610.23048\"}); Total lines: 2886\nNo matching text found for \"2610.23048\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComputation and Language (https://arxiv.org/list/cs.CL/new)\nciteturn41282view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/list/cs.CL/new\",\"pattern\":\"2610.23051\"}); Total lines: 2886\nNo matching text found for \"2610.23051\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComputation and Language (https://arxiv.org/list/cs.CL/new)\nciteturn41282view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/list/cs.CL/new\",\"pattern\":\"2610.23213\"}); Total lines: 2886\nNo matching text found for \"2610.23213\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComputation and Language (https://arxiv.org/list/cs.CL/new)\nciteturn41282view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/list/cs.CL/new\",\"pattern\":\"2610.22820\"}); Total lines: 2886\nL482: Recent studies have shown that position bias remains a critical vulnerability in large language models (LLMs), undermining both the integrity of multiple-choice evaluations and the reliability of retrieval-augmented generation. However, existing understanding of this phenomenon is largely derived from studies with limited model coverage and English-centric benchmarks, leaving the behavior of frontier models and diverse languages underexplored.\nL483: In this work, we conduct a comprehensive investigation of position bias across model scales ranging from billion-level to trillion-level parameters, covering both multiple-choice and multi-document tasks in five languages. Our experiments reveal that while position bias remains noticeable in small-scale models and Non-English languages, increasing model scale and employing standard Chain-of-Thought prompting significantly mitigate this issue.\nL484: Moreover, we demonstrate that emerging \\emph{thinking models} with extended reasoning capabilities are nearly immune to position bias, suggesting a costly but general approach toward resolving this vulnerability.\nL485: [39] cite344†arXiv:2610.22810 [cite345†pdf , cite346†html , cite347†other ]\nL486: \nL487: \nL488: Title: Watermarkability: Measuring and Improving Language-Model Compatibility with Logit-Based Watermarks\nL489: \nL490: cite348†Zewei Deng , cite349†Mohamed Seif , cite350†Liyan Xie L491: \nL492: Comments: 16 pages, 2 figures, 10 tables. Code available at cite351†this https URL†github.com L493: \nL494: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL); Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)\nL495: Language-model watermarks can help identify model-generated text, encode provenance information, and detect post-generation edits. A broad class of logit-based watermarks increases the logits of a designated token set at each decoding step. Because a base model's native probability mass on this set varies across contexts, models, and watermark rules, the same logit bias can yield different adherence rates and increases in perplexity (PPL).\nL496: We introduce watermarkability to characterize the compatibility between a model and the token sets requested by a watermark. At a target mass $\\tau$, we quantify this property through a watermarkability cost: the expected minimum KL divergence from the base-model distribution required to assign probability at least $\\tau$ to the requested set. A lower cost means that the model is more watermarkable for that request distribution. To reduce this cost, we propose a two-stage method.\nL497: In the first stage, we train a target-set-conditioned low-rank adapter on unions drawn from a 16-way vocabulary partition to increase the requested set's probability mass. In the second stage, a closed-form controller applies the smallest remaining uniform logit bias needed to reach the target mass. We evaluate the method on six watermark schedules using OPT-125M and Qwen3-8B.\nL498: Across the 12 model-schedule pairs, the pre-adaptation watermarkability cost correlates with the log-PPL increase under a near-hard fixed-bias baseline (pooled Pearson $r=0.929$). For every schedule, at least 99.80% of sampled tokens fall in the target set requested at their decoding step. At this reliability, the adapter and controller reduce average per-schedule prompt-conditional PPL by 46.3% on OPT-125M and 5.2% on Qwen3-8B relative to the fixed-bias baseline.\nL499: [40] cite352†arXiv:2610.22820 [cite353†pdf , cite354†html , cite355†other ]\nL500: \nL501: \nL502: Title: Causal Edit Serialization\nL503: \nL504: cite356†Louis Robinson L505: \nL506: Comments: 37 pages, including appendices and checklist. Code: cite357†this https URL†github.com ; data and checkpoints: cite358†this https URL†huggingface.co L507: \nL508: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL509: Standard autoregressive language models generate text sequentially, but revision requires referring back to tokens in the input context or those already generated. Causal Edit Serialization (CES) is introduced as a framework for expressing such revisions within the same causal token stream. Special pointer tokens are paired with predicted source addresses, allowing insertion, deletion, replacement, copying and movement to be localized explicitly.\nL510: A lightweight localization head and dual-index rotary position encoding support both address prediction and conditioning on previously emitted pointers, while retaining a standard causal attention mask. A pretraining procedure combines ordinary language modeling with serialized repairs derived from random corruptions, model-generated edits and repeated text.\nL511: Across model scales and editing baselines, CES models demonstrate complete applied edits with modestly higher clean-text bits per byte than clean autoregressive models. Supervised and reinforcement-learning post-training provide a grammatical-error-correction proof of concept, producing more beneficial than harmful changes under reference-blind model judging. Correct repair programs also improve subsequent text prediction while the original context and its cached states are retained.\nL512: CES provides a common interface for generation and localized revision, with potential extensions to iterative refinement and additional pointer-addressed transformations.\nL513: [41] cite359†arXiv:2610.22841 [cite360†pdf , cite361†html , cite362†other ]\nL514: \nL515: \nL516: Title: The Reliance Hypothesis: Chain-of-Thought Faithfulness Rises as Language Resource Level Falls\nL517: \nL518: cite363†Lucas Resck , cite364†Jingyi Sun , cite365†Qianli Wang , cite366†Isabelle Augenstein , cite367†Anna Korhonen L519: \nL520: Comments: 21 pages, 10 figures, 16 tables\nL521: \nL522: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL523: Chain-of-Thought (CoT) faithfulness matters for monitoring, explaining and safely deploying large language models, yet it has been studied mostly in English. Languages differ in level, the amount of text available to learn from, so models know less in lower-resource languages, which may change how much they rely on their reasoning. To our knowledge, we are the first to relate CoT faithfulness to both resource level and task type (knowledge- or reasoning-intensive).\nL524: On parallel multiple-choice questions from Multilingual Massive Multitask Language Understanding (MMMLU) in eight languages, faithfulness rises as resource level falls on knowledge-intensive tasks but not on reasoning-intensive ones, a gap that points the same way across four models and several faithfulness measures. We propose, and find support for, the Reliance Hypothesis: models rely more on their reasoning when they cannot recall the answer, as in lower-resource languages on knowledge-intensive tasks.\nL525: Model confidence partly explains the effect of resource level, but not the knowledge-reasoning gap. Languages in which models are less confident rely more on reasoning, yet less confident answers within a language do not, a Simpson's paradox. In lower-resource languages, models rely on their reasoning for more answers, not more strongly for each. CoT monitoring may therefore be most informative in lower-resource languages, where it is least often validated.\nL526: [42] cite368†arXiv:2610.22858 [cite369†pdf , cite370†html , cite371†other ]\nL527: \nL528: \nL529: Title: Uncertainty-Aware Budget Allocation for Efficient LLM Tree Search\nL530: \nL531: cite372†Jianguo Huang , cite373†Zhiyi Lyu , cite374†Fuxiang Zhang , cite375†Yanchen Deng , cite376†Deheng Ye , cite377†Bo An L532: \nL533: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL534: Tree search enhances multi-step reasoning in large language models (LLMs), yet prevailing methods expand every step with a fixed budget, irrespective of the marginal utility of expansion. We first show, across existing search algorithms, that continuations sampled from a shared intermediate state are largely duplicates and collapse to a few distinct answers.\nL535: Critically, we find that the semantic dispersion of sibling actions predicts the redundancy of their downstream continuations, permitting a subtree's budget to be fixed before expansion. An information-theoretic analysis motivates this: treating the per-step budget as the Monte Carlo sample size for estimating a step's residual answer entropy, we derive a sufficient budget that shrinks with sibling dispersion.\nL536: We accordingly propose $\\textbf{Uncertainty-Aware Allocation}$ (UAA), a training-free framework that decouples value from uncertainty: values determine which candidates are promising, while uncertainty, estimated from sibling dispersion at no additional model cost, determines how many merit expansion, via uncertainty-aware pruning and uncertainty-aware budgeting.\nL537: Across mathematical reasoning, logical reasoning, and planning benchmarks, UAA reduces token usage by up to $90\\%$ and latency by up to $9\\times$ at comparable accuracy, generalizing across four tree search algorithms and both open- and closed-source LLMs.\nL538: [43] cite378†arXiv:2610.22874 [cite379†pdf , cite380†html , cite381†other ]\nL539: \nL540: \nL541: Title: Identify, Classify, Act: Per-Instruction Controlled Compliance for LLMs over External Data\nL542: \nL543: cite382†Zesheng Shi , cite383†Xue Li , cite384†Chenrui Dai , cite254†Jing Li L544: \nL545: Journal-ref: EMNLP2026\nL546: \nL547: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL); Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)\nL548: LLMs over external data face a per-instruction tension: a single document or tool return often interleaves legitimate directives with adversarial prompt injections, and the model must follow the former while rejecting the latter. Existing defenses suppress or trust all external instructions, collapsing the safety-utility frontier and forcing a choice between over-refusal and over-compliance.\nL549: We argue the missing capability is \\textbf{per-instruction credit attribution} and formalize it as External-Data Instruction Classification, an identify-classify-act decision over each embedded directive. We propose \\textbf{EDIC}, a decomposed reward framework for per-instruction compliance.\nL550: A scene-grounded pipeline injects mixed beneficial and harmful instructions with counterfactual filtering, while a generative judge is initialized by SFT and refined with GRPO to attribute compliance at the instruction level. The judge then produces a reward that separately encourages beneficial compliance and penalizes harmful compliance for RL training.\nL551: Across two backbones and five benchmarks, EDIC improves both safety and utility over the strongest prior defense, lifting harmful-rejection by up to 7.7\\% and Arena-Hard-Auto utility by 12.5\\% on Qwen3-4B. The code repository is hosted at cite385†this https URL†github.com .\n"
